<h2>Part A: the 2-year design refresh</h2>

<div class="sop-meta">
  <div><strong>Trigger</strong> Two-year anniversary of the launch date</div>
  <div><strong>Owner</strong> Lead designer / lead dev</div>
  <div><strong>Time</strong> ~30 hrs spread over 2-3 weeks</div>
  <div><strong>Output</strong> Refreshed visual design live, same URLs, same SEO equity</div>
</div>

<h3>Why we do it</h3>
<p>Design ages faster than code. A site that looked great in 2026 looks tired by 2028. A tired-looking site costs leads quietly; the visitor doesn't email to say "your site looks dated," they just leave. The 2-year refresh is included in every Monthly Care Plan and is the single most expensive thing we give away.</p>

<h3>What we redo</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Typography (display + body face)</li>
  <li>Color palette (primary, accent, neutrals)</li>
  <li>Photography style + treatment</li>
  <li>Hero treatment + layout rhythm</li>
  <li>Button style + form style</li>
  <li>Card style + shadow language</li>
  <li>Footer layout</li>
  <li>Iconography</li>
</ul>

<h3>What stays</h3>
<ul>
  <li>URLs (every existing URL still resolves)</li>
  <li>Content (no copy rewrites unless they request)</li>
  <li>Page structure + navigation</li>
  <li>SEO meta (titles, descriptions, schema)</li>
  <li>Tone of voice (unless they explicitly want a re-voicing)</li>
  <li>Domain, email, hosting (no migration)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Refresh process (compressed version of the original build)</h3>

<ul class="sop-checklist">
  <li><strong>Day 0:</strong> send the refresh-kickoff email. Schedule a 20-minute call to confirm scope.</li>
  <li><strong>Days 1-3:</strong> wireframe / direction round. Show 2 design directions, not 1. Let them pick.</li>
  <li><strong>Days 4-10:</strong> build the refresh on a feature branch (Cloudflare Pages preview URL).</li>
  <li><strong>Days 10-12:</strong> internal QA + Lighthouse + accessibility on the preview.</li>
  <li><strong>Days 12-14:</strong> client preview round. Iterate.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 15ish:</strong> launch to production with a single PR merge. No DNS work; everything stays at the same domain.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 15+:</strong> 1-week monitoring, same as a fresh launch.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Refresh-kickoff email</h3>

<div class="sop-template">
  <div class="sop-template-label">2-year refresh kickoff</div>
<pre>Hi {first_name},

Two years since launch (April {year}). Per the monthly plan, your
2-year design refresh is up.

WHAT THIS IS: a visual redesign at no additional cost. Same content,
same URLs, same SEO. New typography, color, photo treatment, layout
rhythm. The wiring stays; the look changes.

WHAT IT ISN'T: a content rewrite or restructure. If you also want
content edits, that's separate (also usually free).

I'll show you 2 design directions next week and you pick. Whole thing
takes about 3 weeks end to end. No work on your end besides feedback.

Want to grab 20 minutes next week to confirm scope?

— Jonathan</pre>
</div>

<h3>Common refresh issues</h3>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Client wants to skip the refresh</strong>
  Fine. Note in CRM. Re-offer in 6 months. Some clients love the original; respect that.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Client wants to "redo everything"</strong>
  The refresh covers visual. Content rewrites or structural changes are separate. Quote those in addition to the free refresh.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout">
  <strong>Client adds new requirements during the refresh</strong>
  Absorb if they're small (a new section, an extra page). Quote separately if substantial (new feature, new integration). Same rules as a fresh build.
</div>

